---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Carousel from '../../components/Carousel.astro';
---

<CaseLayout
  headline="HBS AI Institute is a global research center that provides research-driven insights on using AI to advance business and society."
  intro={[
    'As AI products multiplied around automation and content generation, HBS wanted to explore a more meaningful opportunity for AI in strategy among executives.',
    'The goal was to identify potential users and problems worth pursuing. The work resulted in:',
  ]}
  stakeholder="HBS AI Institute (Previously D^3 Institute)"
  skills={['Research Strategy', 'Product Discovery', 'Qualitative Data Analysis']}
  year="2025 (4 weeks)"
  role="Designer + Researcher"
  teamLabel="Team"
  hero="/assets/hbs/imgPanwImage011.png"
  theme="hbs"
  previousHref="/work/panw-ai/"
  nextHref="/work/one-report/"
>
  <Fragment slot="intro-extra">
    <ol class="figma-list intro-list">
      <li><strong>A defined problem set</strong>, frameworks to interpret and prioritize them, and follow-on product discovery.</li>
      <li><strong>Human–AI interaction principles</strong> used to fine-tune model responses across HBS projects for business executives.</li>
      <li><strong>A research architecture</strong> to quantify qualitative responses at scale for future research projects.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Problem</h2>
    <p>There was no defined industry, executive role, or problem space. “Executives” could mean anyone from a CEO/Chairman to Director/Lead. “Strategy” could include almost every consequential decision they made.</p>
    <p>Without clear boundaries, almost any polished answer could appear complete, even when important situations had been missed.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure
    src="/assets/hbs/imgConsistencyVsInterpretiveDepthTable1.png"
    alt="Illustration of feeling untethered"
    caption="Feeling untethered without a well-defined audience, industry, or problem space. [AI-generated]"
    class="hbs-untethered"
  />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Approach</h2>
    <p class="approach-lead">To avoid narrowing the opportunity around our initial assumptions, I organized the work around three questions:<br /><br /></p>
    <ol class="figma-list approach-list">
      <li>How executives formulate strategy,</li>
      <li>What problems they encounter while doing so, and</li>
      <li>What role AI currently plays in their decisions.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="figma-blank" aria-hidden="true">​</p>
    <p>I gathered credible resources and papers to examined these questions broadly across roles, industries, and types of strategy.</p>
    <p class="figma-blank" aria-hidden="true">​</p>
    <p>To handle research at scale, I built an AI-assisted research architecture that broke 237 papers into 992 traceable modules, translated qualitative observations into comparable measures, and preserved the evidence behind each synthesis.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="research-architecture" data-reveal>
    <img src="/assets/hbs/imgImage2.png" alt="Data collection" loading="lazy" data-lightbox-image tabindex="0" role="button" />
    <img src="/assets/hbs/imgImage3.png" alt="Extraction" loading="lazy" data-lightbox-image tabindex="0" role="button" />
    <img src="/assets/hbs/imgImage4.png" alt="Classification" loading="lazy" data-lightbox-image tabindex="0" role="button" />
    <img src="/assets/hbs/imgImage5.png" alt="Inquiry routing" loading="lazy" data-lightbox-image tabindex="0" role="button" />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Maintaining Standards for AI Outputs</h2>
    <p>Reasoning models gave reliable answers but missed hidden meanings. Creativity models could go beyond basic understanding, but they weren’t consistent in applying logic across different sets.</p>
    <p>Prompt engineering brought everything together. It let us combine the creative and analytical strengths of GPT 4.5 with the steady reliability of o3. I kept the same goal but tried several versions of the AI pipeline.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="media-stack" data-reveal>
    <Figure src="/assets/hbs/imgImage69.png" alt="Model strengths and weaknesses mapping" caption="Mapping current models’ strengths and weaknesses against our target performance." />
    <Figure src="/assets/hbs/imgImage71.png" alt="Consistency and interpretive depth model comparison" caption="Models were compared by consistency and interpretive depth to identify the strongest balance between reliable output and meaningful interpretation." />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing Situational Axes.</h2>
    <p>Despite multiple attempts at sorting, the resulting clusters were still too large to facilitate meaningful collaboration or productive discussion.</p>
    <p>I considered subdividing the clusters by industry; however, this approach risked reinforcing industry stereotypes. Activities within a single industry can differ significantly. For example, marketing within pharmaceuticals and legal operations within a content generation team represent distinct functions.</p>
    <p>To address this challenge, I developed a novel approach for mapping activities. Each activity was evaluated along shared dimensions, including regulation, modularity, timing, value horizon, knowledge transfer, and market spread, using situational axes.</p>
    <p>This approach ensured that each collection was mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive, allowing every module to be mapped without exception.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure src="/assets/hbs/imgPanwImage012.png" alt="Situational tags applied to research modules" caption="Tagging each module based on the situations influencing the activity." />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <p>With clusters finally manageable, I used contrastive typology: scoring evidence along bipolar situational dimensions and comparing their polar intersections to identify distinct operating contexts.</p>
    <p>Although the dimensions produced several plausible permutations, collaborative working sessions helped us eliminate factors that did not generate meaningful distinctions, felt insufficiently relevant to the team’s objectives, or were unlikely to support useful product decisions.</p>
  </section>

  <Figure src="/assets/hbs/imgImage32.png" alt="Four situational axes" caption="Four situational axes created a common language for comparing otherwise unrelated forms of strategic work." class="caption-spacious" />

  <Carousel
    slides={['/assets/hbs/imgImage79.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage80.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage81.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage82.png']}
    startIndex={1}
    aspectRatio="680 / 388"
    data-reveal
  >
    <p>I used the matrix to show HBS four ways to narrow the opportunity for further discovery—by recurring problem, operating context, a combination of both, or one focused starting point.</p>
  </Carousel>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Designing Principles of Human-AI interaction</h2>
    <p>While not part of the scope, I recognized we had rich data, capable of helping us define design principles for AI interactions among executives for strategic discussions.</p>
    <p>This allowed us to test different scenarios and contexts, helping us fine-tune the voice of an AI system designed specifically for executives.</p>
  </section>

  <Carousel
    slides={['/assets/hbs/imgImage78.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage72.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage73.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage74.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage75.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage76.png', '/assets/hbs/imgImage77.png']}
    startIndex={1}
    aspectRatio="680 / 385"
    data-reveal
  >
    <p>Design principles often sound good but aren’t actually useful. When they become truisms like “AI should be trustworthy,” “prioritize the user,” or “support human judgment,” they’re easy to agree with but hard to use in real design work.</p>
    <p>Having many modules in Research Question 3 let us dig deeper and find hidden meanings. This helped me develop a way to frame principles as thoughtful trade-offs, grounded in context rather than as absolute truths.</p>
    <p>I wrote each principle so its opposite could also make sense; something a smart team might choose in another situation or with different goals.</p>
  </Carousel>

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Impact:</h2>
    <p class="body-regular">The work continued in three ways.</p>
    <ol class="figma-numbered-list">
      <li><strong>The seven human-AI interaction principles were used to fine-tune model responses across exploratory HBS projects for business executives.</strong> They gave teams specific behaviors to compare. For example, whether a model should present its conclusion or reasoning first, accept the user’s framing or introduce another perspective, and state uncertainty directly or leave it implicit.</li>
      <li><strong>The research architecture was repurposed for other qualitative work.</strong><br />We used it whenever it was necessary to converted unstructured text into comparable measures and reviewable patterns. Two immediate examples included scoring participant-written prompts at scale for HBS’  Sana.ai prompt engineering course, and analyze open-ended surveys from Leading with AI events.</li>
      <li><strong>The sixteen people problems initiated follow-on research and exploration across HBS teams.</strong> Instead of beginning with “executive strategy” as one enormous subject, teams could investigate specific tensions such as deployment versus governance, short-term gains versus long-term capability, or intuitive judgment versus structured analysis.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <Figure src="/assets/hbs/imgImage68.png" alt="Research architecture reused across HBS" caption="Research architecture repurposed for additional qualitative analysis across the org." />

  <section class="case-section reading-column" data-reveal>
    <h2>Lessons</h2>
    <p class="body-regular">The hardest part was distinguishing genuine insight from plausible-looking output.</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson #1: AI forced me to become more precise about research.</strong></p>
    <p class="body-regular">To get precise outputs, a lot of effort went into defining, and revisiting terms we often take for granted. Like, ‘What makes an insight “insightful”?’, ‘Which form of ‘thematic synthesis’ is most applicable for a problem?’</p>
    <p><strong>Lesson #2: At scale, AI-assisted research can very quickly turn into a pipeline management activity.</strong></p>
    <p class="body-regular">Once hundreds of sources moved through the study, prompts, definitions, scoring rules, model choices, and review checkpoints became part of the research method.</p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

